WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
The first film left IP3 traveling and DAG in the membrane.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.180
How can conventional protein kinase C use both branches?

3
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.920
The branches reach the same cell but keep different locations.

4
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.800
Return to the same cell and follow one kinase.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.500
Follow its movement and timing.

6
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:27.173
At rest, conventional PKC is folded shut.

7
00:00:28.493 --> 00:00:33.273
Its pseudosubstrate occupies the kinase active site, blocking real substrates.

8
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.173
All four parts are connected in one protein chain.

9
00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:40.873
A C2 domain can respond to calcium.

10
00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:44.393
A C1 domain can recognize DAG.

11
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:51.447
IP3 opened the endoplasmic reticulum channel, raising cytosolic calcium.

12
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:56.207
Calcium binds C2 and changes its membrane affinity.

13
00:00:57.587 --> 00:01:03.567
C2 then engages anionic lipids, bringing conventional PKC toward the plasma

14
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:08.427
membrane. The kinase is now close enough to sample the local membrane.

15
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:13.070
At the membrane, C1 can search for DAG.

16
00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:18.810
DAG binding strengthens membrane engagement and helps displace the pseudosubstrate.

17
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:24.550
The active site opens, so the kinase can phosphorylate protein substrates.

18
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:32.313
Now compare three schematic conditions: calcium without DAG, DAG without raised

19
00:01:32.313 --> 00:01:34.573
calcium, and both branches together.

20
00:01:35.853 --> 00:01:40.513
Which condition should retain conventional PKC most strongly at this membrane?

21
00:01:46.617 --> 00:01:50.917
Each contact alone can be weak under physiological conditions.

22
00:01:52.397 --> 00:01:56.717
Together, their binding energies add, so membrane preference can rise sharply.

23
00:01:58.137 --> 00:02:04.877
Conventional PKC gives a stronger, longer response where calcium targeting and DAG

24
00:02:04.877 --> 00:02:06.077
activation overlap.

25
00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.470
This is not a perfectly simultaneous digital switch.

26
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:16.510
Calcium can recruit the kinase rapidly, before DAG engages C1.

27
00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:19.930
The response is graded, not all or none.

28
00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:23.810
The detector integrates timing as well as place.

29
00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:27.613
The rule belongs to conventional PKC.

30
00:02:28.993 --> 00:02:34.813
Novel PKC has a C1 module suited to DAG-driven recruitment without calcium.

31
00:02:36.313 --> 00:02:41.653
Atypical PKC responds to neither messenger and instead uses protein partners.

32
00:02:42.817 --> 00:02:48.197
A phorbol ester can occupy the DAG site and activate PKC directly.

33
00:02:49.557 --> 00:02:51.637
Unlike DAG, it is cleared slowly.

34
00:02:53.057 --> 00:02:58.777
Prolonged activation can lead to kinase degradation, so signal duration changes the

35
00:02:58.777 --> 00:02:59.777
outcome.

36
00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:02.207
Return once more to the membrane.

37
00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:05.907
Calcium changes C2-dependent targeting.

38
00:03:07.427 --> 00:03:11.047
DAG changes C1-dependent activation and retention.

39
00:03:12.407 --> 00:03:19.147
Keep this rule: conventional PKC integrates calcium targeting with DAG activation at

40
00:03:19.147 --> 00:03:19.507
membranes.
